def add_questions(q):
    q('q49','Count events, keep the exposure','Likelihood','Medium','researcher',10,
      'Four independent exponential waiting times, with a common unknown rate λ > 0, are fully observed: 1, 2, 3 and 4 seconds. Derive the likelihood, verify its maximum, and report the maximum likelihood rate in events per second.', .4,
      ['An exponential density is λ exp(−λx) for x ≥ 0.', 'Sum the exposure times before differentiating the log-likelihood.'],
      'The joint density is λ^4 exp(−10λ), and the log-likelihood is 4 log λ − 10λ. Its derivative is 4/λ − 10, zero at λ = 0.4. The second derivative −4/λ² is negative, and the likelihood approaches zero at both limits. Thus this is the unique global maximum, in inverse seconds. The mean wait is 2.5 seconds; the rate is its reciprocal.',
      'Now suppose only two events were observed over 10 seconds of total exposure, with the other waits independently right-censored. Derive the rate estimate. What changes if no events occur?',
      'Each event supplies a density λ exp(−λx); each censored observation supplies survival exp(−λc). The likelihood is proportional to λ² exp(−10λ), so the MLE is 0.2 per second. With no events it is exp(−10λ), decreasing on λ > 0: the supremum is at the excluded boundary λ → 0, so no positive-rate MLE exists. Informative censoring requires a different observation model.', 'l25')
    q('q50','The derivative missed the support','Likelihood','Hard','researcher',12,
      'Let X₁,…,Xₙ be iid uniform on [0,θ], θ > 0, n ≥ 1. Use the density f(x;θ)=1/θ for 0 ≤ x ≤ θ and zero otherwise. Your observed maximum M is positive. A colleague differentiates −n log θ, finds no stationary point, and concludes that no MLE exists. Diagnose the error, derive the MLE, and decide whether it is unbiased.', None,
      ['Could a proposed endpoint below an observed value have generated the record?', 'Optimize on the feasible support, then use the distribution of the sample maximum to check bias.'],
      'The full likelihood is θ^(−n) times the indicator that θ ≥ M. It is zero below M and decreases on [M,∞), so the MLE is M using the specified closed-support density. There is no need for an interior stationary point. Under the model P(M ≤ m)=(m/θ)^n on [0,θ], so E[M]=nθ/(n+1). M is downward biased; (n+1)M/n is unbiased but is not the likelihood maximizer. Likelihood fit and unbiasedness are different criteria.',
      'What if the density convention instead excludes x=θ? Does this change the probability law or the literal existence of a likelihood maximizer?',
      'The probability law is unchanged because endpoints have probability zero. With the strict-support density θ > M, the same supremum is approached as θ decreases to M but is not attained. This subtlety is why the density version and parameter domain were specified. Regular asymptotic MLE arguments should not be applied automatically to a parameter-dependent support.', 'l25')
    q('q51','One observation copied a hundred times','Likelihood','Hard','researcher',12,
      'One Bernoulli draw Y with unknown success probability p is observed to be 1. A pipeline copies it into 100 identical rows and fits an iid Bernoulli model. Compare the correct and naive likelihoods. Do their MLEs differ on [0,1]? Does agreement of the MLE mean the inferred precision is valid?', None,
      ['There was one random draw. The copies are deterministic given it.', 'Compare likelihood ratios at p=0.5 and p=1, not only the location of the maximum.'],
      'The correct likelihood is p, because the whole copied record occurs exactly when Y=1. The naive iid likelihood is p^100. Both maximize at p=1 on [0,1], but the likelihood ratio of 0.5 to 1 is 0.5 in the real model and 2^(−100) in the naive one. Copying created apparent concentration without information. The endpoint also means an interior normal approximation is unjustified. Identical fitted estimates can hide a radically wrong uncertainty model.',
      'In market data, distinct rows may still be dependent. How would you improve the model without claiming that every repeated value is a duplicate?',
      'Identify the observation unit and sampling mechanism first. Distinguish true duplicate records from distinct events with identical values. A joint model may factor into conditional probabilities given history rather than marginal iid probabilities. Fit and validate an appropriate dependence model; use resampling or robust uncertainty methods only with assumptions matching that dependence. Row count alone is not effective sample size.', 'l25')
